Document (dev.stxt.website): Streaming logs | STXT

	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-20
		Description: STXT for log files: one entry per root node, multi-line messages and stack traces with no escape characters, template validation and streaming reads, entry by entry.

	Header: Streaming logs

	Assert >>
		A log is written at the end and read from start to finish.
		In @STXT@ each entry is a root node, and the file is processed entry by entry, without loading it whole.

	Subheader: A log file

	Code >>
		Log (com.acme.log): Start of user creation
			Time: 2026-09-20T10:15:02.120Z
			Level: INFO
			Request ID: 3e45bad6-3a82-4959-844e-9eefd4c418a3
			Message >>
				Creating the user:
				- Name:
				- Age: 19

		Log (com.acme.log): Error creating user
			Time: 2026-09-20T10:15:02.480Z
			Level: ERROR
			Request ID: 3e45bad6-3a82-4959-844e-9eefd4c418a3
			Message: Cannot invoke "String.length()" because "name" is null
			Stacktrace >>
				Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "String.length()" because "name" is null
					at com.example.service.UserService.validateName(UserService.java:42)
					at com.example.service.UserService.createUser(UserService.java:27)
					at com.example.controller.UserController.register(UserController.java:58)
					at com.example.App.main(App.java:15)

		Log (com.acme.log): Request rejected
			Time: 2026-09-20T10:15:02.495Z
			Level: WARN
			Request ID: 3e45bad6-3a82-4959-844e-9eefd4c418a3
			Message: 400 Bad Request

	Content >>
		In this example we have:

		* **Entries**: each `Log` is a root node, independent of the others. The namespace is not
		  inherited between root nodes, which is why each entry declares its own.
		* **Data**: `Time`, `Level` and `Request ID`. They are nodes with a value, which a program can filter.
		* **Text**: `Message` and `Stacktrace`. In a block node, quotes, `:` and line breaks
		  are written as they are, with no escape characters.
		* **Writing**: adding an entry is writing at the end of the file. There is no closing to
		  maintain, and no separators between entries.

	Subheader: The template

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.log
			Structure >>
				Log:
					Time: (1) TIMESTAMP
					Level: (1) ENUM [DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR]
					Request ID: (?) UUID
					Message: (1) TEXT
					Stacktrace: (?) TEXT

	Subsubheader: An entry that does not validate

	Code >>
		# ERROR: this document does not validate
		Log (com.acme.log): Error creating user
			# A TIMESTAMP is ISO 8601: `2026-09-20T10:15:02Z`
			Time: 20/09/2026 10:15
			# Lowercase `error` is not `ERROR`
			Level: error
			# Not a UUID
			Request ID: 3e45bad6
			# `Message` is missing, and it is required

	Subheader: Reading in streaming

	Content >>
		The parser delivers each root node when it is complete and already validated, and then releases it.
		The memory in use is that of one entry, not that of the file.
		This program shows the entries of level `ERROR`:

	Python >>
		from stxt import Parser, StreamObserver, UnifiedSchemaProvider, SchemaValidator

		class Errors(StreamObserver):
		    def on_root_node(self, log):
		        if log.get_child("Level").get_text() == "ERROR":
		            print(log.get_child("Time").get_text(), log.get_text())
		    def on_error(self, error):
		        print("line", error.line, error.code)

		provider = UnifiedSchemaProvider()
		with open(".stxt/log.stxt", encoding="utf-8") as f:
		    provider.add_file(f.read())

		parser = Parser(max_input_size=-1)
		parser.register_validator(SchemaValidator(provider))
		parser.register_stream_observer(Errors())
		with open("app.stxt", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as f:
		    parser.parse_stream(f)

	Listing >>
		2026-09-20T10:15:02.480Z Error creating user

	Content >>
		* An invalid entry does not stop the read: its errors arrive through `on_error`,
		  and the other entries keep being delivered.
		* The size of the input is limited by default to 10,000,000 characters
		  ([STXT-SPEC §11.2](stxt-core-ref#s11-2)). A log may exceed it, which is why the example
		  disables the limit with `max_input_size=-1`.
		* [TypeScript](tools-typescript) and [Java](tools-java) have the same API: `parseStream` and `StreamObserver`.
		* `stxt validate` also reads in streaming: `stxt validate app.stxt --max-input-size -1`
		  validates a file of any size ([The command line](tools-cli)).

	Subsubheader: A truncated file

	Content >>
		If the process dies halfway through a write, the file ends in an incomplete entry:

	Listing >>
		Log (com.acme.log): Request rejected
			Time: 2026-09-20T10:15:02.495Z
			Lev

	Content >>
		The errors stay in that entry: an `INVALID_LINE` on the truncated line, and a
		`TOO_FEW_CHILDREN` for each required node that is missing. The previous entries are read all the same.

	Subheader: Writing entries

	Content >>
		An entry is built as a tree, and `NodeWriter` writes it.
		In this example the message comes from outside, and tries to forge an entry:

	Python >>
		from stxt import InlineNode, NodeWriter

		log = InlineNode("Log", "com.acme.log", "Login failed")
		log.add_inline_node("Time", "2026-09-20T10:16:40Z")
		log.add_inline_node("Level", "WARN")
		log.add_text_node("Message", "User: ana\nLog (com.acme.log): Login succeeded\n\tLevel: INFO")

		with open("app.stxt", "a", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as f:
		    f.write(NodeWriter.to_stxt(log))

	Content >>
		And this is what ends up in the file:

	Code >>
		Log (com.acme.log): Login failed
			Time: 2026-09-20T10:16:40Z
			Level: WARN
			Message >>
				User: ana
				Log (com.acme.log): Login succeeded
					Level: INFO

	Content >>
		* The text of a block node is written indented under its node, and inside a block nothing
		  is interpreted. The forged entry is text of `Message`.
		* An inline value with a line break is rejected with `LINE_BREAK_NOT_ALLOWED`.

	Subheader: Limits

	Content >>
		* An entry takes several lines. `grep ERROR` finds the line, not the entry:
		  filtering by entries takes a program like the one above.
		* A file takes more space than with a one-line-per-entry format.
